    <p><strong>Plovdiv, Bulgaria | July 7–10, 2026</strong> — Five researchers representing five partner institutions represented LifeWatch Slovenia at the LifeWatch ERIC Biodiversity and Ecosystem eScience Conference (<a href="https://www.lifewatch.eu/bees-2026-programme/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">BEeS 2026</a>) in Plovdiv, Bulgaria. The team presented pioneering research spanning subterranean climate vulnerability, citizen science monitoring, species habitat mapping, and remote sensing.</p>
    <h2>Key Contributions &amp; Presentations</h2>
    <h3>Subterranean Biodiversity &amp; Hydrological Dynamics</h3>
    <p>Dr. Magdalena Năpăruş-Aljančič (Karst Research Institute ZRC SAZU) presented <em>“Is the hidden global distribution of subterranean biodiversity hotspots vulnerable to climate change?”</em> in the session <em>Biodiversity and ecosystem responses to climate change</em>. Her models highlighted where exceptional underground biodiversity faces the highest exposure to future climatic shifts. Additionally, Dr. Năpăruş-Aljančič and colleague Žan Kafol (Karst Research Institute ZRC SAZU) led a LifeWatch ERIC Masterclass introducing <strong>FloodWatch</strong>, a web application designed to monitor hydrological dynamics in Slovenia’s karst wetlands and streamline complex environmental data exploration.</p>
    <h3>Habitat Mapping &amp; Olm Conservation</h3>
    <p>Gregor Aljančič (Tular Cave Laboratory) delivered <em>“Preliminary predictive distribution model for <i>Proteus anguinus</i> across the Dinaric Karst”</em> within the <em>Habitat Mapping</em> session, detailing potential current and future distribution models for the olm (<i>Proteus anguinus</i>).</p>
