    <h3>Acoustics, Monitoring and Citizen Science</h3>
    <p>In the <em>Biodiversity observatories and environmental monitoring</em> session, researchers from the National Institute of Biology and the University of Ljubljana showcased novel monitoring approaches:</p>
    <p><strong>Sara Strah</strong> presented <em>“Passive Acoustic Monitoring of Amphibian Biodiversity Using Citizen Science,”</em> drawing on an extensive dataset of more than 2,500 recording hours.</p>
    <p><strong>Matic Gabor</strong> presented <em>“Towards pheromone-based monitoring of the great capricorn beetle.”</em></p>
    <p>Both Strah and Gabor participated in the conference BioBlitz, where Strah earned second prize.</p>
    <h3>Forest Hydrological Modelling &amp; Session Leadership</h3>
    <p>Saša Šercer (Slovenian Forestry Institute) chaired the <em>Biodiversity Observatories &amp; Environmental Monitoring</em> session and presented her research, <em>“Integrating in-situ and remote sensing data for water budget modelling in forested karst ecosystems,”</em> in the <em>Modelling for biodiversity and ecosystems</em> track.</p>
    <h2>Global Scientific Exchange</h2>
    <p>BEeS 2026 gathered biodiversity researchers and data modellers from 28 countries, offering an active platform for knowledge exchange, networking, and international collaboration. LifeWatch Slovenia looks forward to building on these connections at future LifeWatch ERIC events.</p>
